      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"myglobal\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [18, 8, 18]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 3.40 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet's analyze the provided code and defect report step by step to determine if the reported atomicity violation is valid.\n\n### Step 1: **Check Control Conditions**\n\n#### Function `svp_simple_511_001_isr_1`\n- **Line 8 (`myglobal++`)**:\n  - This operation is inside the ISR `svp_simple_511_001_isr_1`.\n  - It is guarded by a `lock()` call on line 7 and followed by an `unlock()` call on line 9.\n  - There are no additional control conditions (like `if`, `while`, etc.) guarding this operation. The `lock()` ensures that only one thread or interrupt can execute this critical section at a time.\n  - Therefore, the write operation on line 8 can be executed whenever the ISR is triggered and the lock is acquired.\n\n#### Function `svp_simple_511_001_main`\n- **Line 18 (`myglobal++`)**:\n  - This operation is inside the main function.\n  - It is guarded by a `lock()` call on line 17 and followed by an `unlock()` call on line 19.\n  - Similar to the ISR, there are no additional control conditions guarding this operation. The `lock()` ensures mutual exclusion while executing this critical section.\n  - Therefore, the write operation on line 18 can be executed during the normal execution of the main function when the lock is acquired.\n\n### Step 2: **Pay Attention to Control Condition Changes**\n- Both the ISR and the main function use `lock()` and `unlock()` to ensure mutual exclusion.\n- There are no changes in control conditions that could alter the truth value of any guards around the read/write operations.\n\n### Step 3: **Check Interrupt Status**\n- The `lock()` and `unlock()` functions manage access to the critical sections.\n- The `lock()` function likely disables interrupts or uses a similar mechanism to prevent preemption during the critical section.\n- In both the ISR and the main function, the `lock()` ensures that no other context (main or ISR) can preempt and modify `myglobal` simultaneously.\n- Since the critical sections are protected by locks, the read/write operations cannot occur simultaneously from different contexts.\n\n### Summary\n- **Line 8 (`myglobal++` in ISR)**:\n  - Can be executed when the ISR is triggered and the lock is acquired.\n- **Line 18 (`myglobal++` in main)**:\n  - Can be executed during the normal execution of the main function when the lock is acquired.\n\n### Atomicity Violation Analysis\n- The reported atomicity violation involves the sequence of operations on lines 18, 8, and 18.\n- However, due to the presence of locks (`lock()` and `unlock()`) around these operations, they are protected from concurrent access.\n- The locks ensure that only one context can execute the critical section at a time, preventing any race conditions or atomicity violations.\n\n### Final Report\n```plaintext\nNo defects.\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```plaintext\nNo defects.\n```\n\n"
